"""Lower Strategy — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Book Three — The Lower Strategy'
HEAD = '''Thirty-four passages, and not one quotation from the <i>Military Prognostications</i> or the <i>Military Dispositions</i>. The <i>Lower Strategy</i> speaks throughout in its own voice, and it has stopped talking about war. Its subject is that when the worthy leave, the state is finished. The <i>Middle Strategy</i> said that this book “expounds the Way and virtue, examines safety and danger, and makes plain the guilt of injuring the worthy,” and all three are here: virtue and music at the opening; the Way, virtue, humanity, duty and ceremony as one body, and command, order and government as three stages of one thing, in the middle; and at the end four clauses on the worthy — “injure a worthy man and the harm reaches three generations; screen a worthy man and it falls on your own person; envy a worthy man and your name will not survive whole; advance a worthy man and the good fortune flows down to your grandchildren.” Somewhere in the middle stands the only place in the whole book where it says plainly why one fights at all: “The sage king’s use of arms is not for the love of it, but to punish the violent and put down disorder… Weapons are instruments of ill omen, which the way of heaven abhors; to use them only when there is no choice — that is the way of heaven.” The second half of that is almost verbatim <i>Laozi</i> 31. Zheng Yuan called this plagiarism from Laozi. Put the other way: the <i>Three Strategies</i> is Laozi carried into a book about armies. One external fact belongs to this scroll in particular: <b>the edict of AD 51 preserved in the biography of Zang Gong quotes the sixth, seventh and eighth passages below verbatim and consecutively</b>, under the title <i>Record of the Yellow Stone Elder</i> — the hardest evidence there is for dating the <i>Three Strategies</i> falls in the <i>Lower Strategy</i>, not the <i>Upper</i>.'''

PARA = {
 'j3-1': 'He who can hold up what is falling under heaven will hold what is secure under heaven; he who can take away the sorrow of the world will enjoy the world’s pleasure; he who can rescue the world from calamity will get the world’s good fortune.',
 'j3-2': 'So when the benefit reaches the people, worthy men come over; when it reaches the insects, sages come over. Where worthy men come, the state is strong; where sages come, the six directions are made one. (“Reaching the insects” is the old figure for humanity carried as far as it will go.)',
 'j3-3': 'Seek the worthy with virtue; bring the sage with the Way. Let the worthy go and the state grows small; let the sage go and the state goes crooked. Small is the stair to danger; crooked is the sign of ruin. (The base text writes “sign” with the graph its simplified form collapses into; both witnesses have the right one.)',
 'j3-4': 'The government of the worthy brings men down in the body; the government of the sage brings them down in the heart. The body brought down, you can begin with them; the heart brought down, you can finish with them. The body is brought down by ceremony, the heart by music.',
 'j3-5': 'And by music is not meant metal, stone, silk and bamboo: it means that men take pleasure in their families, in their clans, in their work, in their towns, in the government’s orders, in the Way and virtue. Then the ruler of men composes music to regulate it, so that it does not lose its measure. (Six “take pleasure in,” from the household outward. The passage turns <i>music</i> into <i>gladness</i>, and it is the most finely made thing in the book.)',
 'j3-6': 'So the lord who has virtue uses music to gladden others; the lord who has none uses music to gladden himself. He who gladdens others lasts long; he who gladdens himself does not last and perishes.',
 'j3-7': 'To leave what is near and plan for what is far is labour without result; to leave what is far and plan for what is near is ease with an end to it. An easy government has many loyal ministers; a laborious government has many resentful subjects.',
 'j3-8': 'Hence: he whose whole aim is to enlarge his territory lays it waste; he whose whole aim is to enlarge his virtue grows strong. He who can keep what is his own is safe; he who covets what is another’s destroys. A government of destruction is paid for by generations; work carried beyond the measure will fail even if it is finished.',
 'j3-9': 'To set oneself aside and instruct others is going against the grain; to set oneself right and so transform others is going with it. Against the grain invites disorder; with it is the essence of good order.',
 'j3-10': 'The Way, virtue, humanity, duty and ceremony — these five are one body. The Way is what men tread; virtue is what men obtain in themselves; humanity is what men hold dear; duty is what men owe; ceremony is what men carry out in their persons. Not one of them can be missing. (Ten of the twelve places where the base text writes “one” with the formal banker’s graph are in this scroll; both witnesses have the plain graph, and it is restored. This passage is the frame of the <i>Lower Strategy</i>, and the ground on which Zhen Dexiu said the book “does not run counter to what the Confucians mean.”)',
 'j3-11': 'So rising early and going late to bed is the discipline of ceremony; putting down a rebel and avenging a wrong is the decision of duty; the heart that cannot bear another’s suffering is the working of humanity; getting for oneself and getting for others is the road of virtue; keeping men level and each in his place is the transforming work of the Way. (Ceremony, duty, humanity, virtue, the Way — the order of the passage before, reversed.)',
 'j3-12': 'What issues from the lord and goes down to the minister is called a command; what is set down on bamboo and silk is called an order; what is taken up and carried out is called government.',
 'j3-13': 'Let the command fail and the order does not run; the order not running, government is not upright; government not upright, the Way does not get through; the Way not getting through, the crooked ministers prevail; the crooked prevailing, the ruler’s authority is injured. (For “upright” both witnesses read “established.”)',
 'j3-14': 'To go a thousand li to meet a worthy man is a long road; to fetch an unfit one is a short road. And so the enlightened king leaves the near and takes the far, and can therefore bring a work to completion and raise men up, and those below him give their whole strength.',
 'j3-15': 'Abolish one good and every good is weakened; reward one evil and every evil comes home. Let the good have their protection and the evil their punishment, and the state is secure and every good comes to it.',
 'j3-16': 'Where the many are in doubt there is no settled state; where the many are confused there is no governing the people. Let the doubt be settled and the confusion undone, and the state can be made secure.',
 'j3-17': 'One order against the grain, and a hundred orders come to nothing; one evil act carried out, and a hundred evils are knotted together. So let good be done to a compliant people and severity fall on a violent one, and the order runs without complaint.',
 'j3-18': 'To govern the aggrieved by means of the aggrieved is called going against heaven; to govern the vengeful by means of the vengeful is a calamity past saving. Govern the people so as to make them level, and reach that levelness by cleanness, and the people will each have their place and the world will be at rest.',
 'j3-19': 'Where those who offend against their superiors are honoured and the greedy and base are rich, even a sage king could not bring about good government.',
 'j3-20': 'Where those who offend are put to death and the greedy and base are put away, the work of transformation runs and every evil is dissolved.',
 'j3-21': 'Men of clean conduct are not to be got with rank and salary; men of principle are not to be frightened with punishments. (The Siku text inverts “punishments and awe.”)',
 'j3-22': 'So the enlightened lord, in seeking the worthy, must first see by what a given man can be brought, and go about it that way. To bring a man of clean conduct, cultivate your ceremony; to bring a man of principle, cultivate your Way. Then the officers can be brought, and your name can be kept.',
 'j3-23': 'The sage and the gentleman understand where rise and decline come from, see through to the beginnings of success and failure, make out the pivot of order and disorder, and know the right moment to take office or to leave it.',
 'j3-24': 'However hard pressed, they will not take a post in a state that is falling; however poor, they will not eat the salary of a lawless country. (Both witnesses read “the grain of a lawless country.” Compare <i>Analects</i> VIII: “do not enter a state in peril, do not dwell in a state in disorder.”)',
 'j3-25': 'He who keeps his name hidden and holds to the Way moves when the hour comes, and may reach the height a subject can reach; and when the virtue is matched in himself, may set up an achievement that stands apart. So his Way is high and his name is known to later ages.',
 'j3-26': 'The sage king’s use of arms is not for the love of it; it is to punish the violent and put down disorder. (The three passages that follow are the only place in the book where it says outright why one fights.)',
 'j3-27': 'To punish the unrighteous with righteousness is like breaching a river to put out a torch, or like pushing at the edge of a chasm a man who is falling already: the thing must succeed.',
 'j3-28': 'That one nevertheless goes slowly and does not press on is because one grudges the further harm to men and things. Weapons are instruments of ill omen, which the way of heaven abhors; to use them only when there is no choice — that is the way of heaven. (The last part is almost verbatim <i>Laozi</i> 31.)',
 'j3-29': 'A man is in the Way as a fish is in water: with water he lives, without it he dies. So the gentleman is always in dread of failing the Way. (Both witnesses read simply “the gentleman is always in dread,” without two of the base text’s words.)',
 'j3-30': 'Let the powerful hold the offices and the state’s authority weakens; let life and death lie with the powerful and the state’s strength is drained; let the powerful bow their heads and the state can last; let life and death lie with the ruler and the state can be secure. Let the four orders of the people run empty and the state has nothing laid by; let them have enough and the state is at peace. (The base text has an unintelligible graph for “empty”; both witnesses have the right one, which answers “have enough” in the next clause.)',
 'j3-31': 'With worthy ministers inside, the crooked are outside; with the crooked inside, the worthy are destroyed. Inside and outside wrongly placed, and the disorder is handed down for generations.',
 'j3-32': 'When great ministers suspect the ruler, every kind of treachery gathers. When a minister occupies the ruler’s honour, high and low are confounded; when the ruler does what a minister should, high and low lose their order.',
 'j3-33': 'Injure a worthy man and the harm reaches three generations; screen a worthy man and it falls on your own person; envy a worthy man and your name will not survive whole; advance a worthy man and the good fortune flows down to your grandchildren. So the gentleman is urgent in advancing worthy men, and a good name comes to him of itself.',
 'j3-34': 'Benefit one and harm a hundred, and the people leave the city walls; benefit one and harm ten thousand, and the state will think of scattering. Take away one and a hundred are the better for it, and men admire the favour; take away one and ten thousand are the better for it, and the government is not disordered. (The book ends here — not on war, but on the arithmetic of one man against ten thousand. All four occurrences of “one” are written with the banker’s graph in the base text and with the plain one in both witnesses and in the Fushimi printing’s last leaf.)',
}
